.rec-grid {{
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(320px, 1fr));
  gap: 16px;
  margin-bottom: 20px;
}}

.rec-card {{
  background: var(--card-bg);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  padding: 22px 20px;
  box-shadow: var(--shadow);
  border-top: 4px solid var(--accent);
}}

.rec-card .rec-num {{
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--accent);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1px;
  margin-bottom: 4px;
}}

.rec-card .rec-title {{
  font-size: 1.05rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  margin-bottom: 6px;
  color: var(--text);
}}

.rec-card .rec-body {{
  font-size: 0.9rem;
  color: #444;
  line-height: 1.6;
}}

.rec-card .rec-source {{
  font-size: 0.78rem;
  color: var(--accent);
  font-weight: 600;
  margin-top: 8px;
}}

/* ── Footer ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
.footer {{
  text-align: center;
  padding: 32px 0;
  color: #999;
  font-size: 0.82rem;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  margin-top: 12px;
}}

.footer p {{ margin-bottom: 4px; }}

/* ── Summary Stat Row ─────────────────────────────────── */
.stat-row {{
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 12px 24px;
  margin-bottom: 16px;
  font-size: 0.92rem;
  color: #444;
}}

.stat-row span {{ white-space: nowrap; }}
.stat-row strong {{ color: var(--text); }}

/* ── Correlation highlight ────────────────────────────── */
.corr-display {{
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
  background: #F8F9FF;
  border: 1px solid #E0E4F0;
  border-radius: 6px;
  padding: 6px 14px;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 1.1rem;
}}

.corr-display.neg {{ color: var(--nonfiction); }}
.corr-display.pos {{ color: #27AE60; }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- ═══════════ HERO ═══════════ -->
<div class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Amazon Bestselling Books<br>Price vs. User Rating Trend Analysis (2009–2019)</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive analysis of how book pricing strategies relate to customer satisfaction across Fiction and Non-Fiction genres over a decade of Amazon bestseller data.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Dataset: 550 records</span>
      <span>Timeframe: 2009–2019</span>
      <span>Prepared: May 2026</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 1: MARKET OVERVIEW ═══════════ -->
<section id="market-overview">
  <h2 class="section-title">1. Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</h2>
  <p class="section-objective">A high-level summary of the dataset: total books, genre split, average price, average rating, and review volume across the full decade.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{rd['total_records']}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Records</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{rd['unique_books']}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Unique Books</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{rd['unique_authors']}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Unique Authors</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{rd['avg_rating']}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. User Rating (0–5)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">${rd['avg_price']:.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Average Book Price</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{format_num(rd['avg_reviews'])}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Review Count</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-block">
      <img src="{charts['genre_pie']}" alt="Genre Distribution Pie Chart">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 1: Genre split across all 550 bestseller records. Non-Fiction dominates at {rd['nonfiction_pct']}%.</div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The dataset spans <strong>{rd['years_range']}</strong> ({rd['total_records']} records) with <strong>{rd['unique_books']} unique titles</strong> from <strong>{rd['unique_authors']} authors</strong>. The average book has a user rating of <strong>{rd['avg_rating']}/5.0</strong> and is priced at <strong>${rd['avg_price']:.2f}</strong> (median: ${rd['median_price']:.2f}).</p>
        <p><strong>Non-Fiction</strong> accounts for {rd['nonfiction_pct']}% of bestseller appearances ({rd['nonfiction_count']} records) compared to <strong>Fiction</strong> at {rd['fiction_pct']}% ({rd['fiction_count']} records). This 56/44 split reflects non-fiction's broader appeal across self-help, biography, reference, and children's categories.</p>
        <p>Ratings are concentrated in the 4.3–4.9 range (μ={rd['avg_rating']}, σ={rd['rating_std']}), consistent with the survivorship bias inherent in bestseller data — these are already market-validated titles.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-row">
        <span>Price range: <strong>$0 – $105</strong></span>
        <span>Rating std. dev: <strong>{rd['rating_std']}</strong></span>
        <span>Median price: <strong>${rd['median_price']:.2f}</strong></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 2: PRICE-RATING CORRELATION ═══════════ -->
<section id="price-rating-correlation">
  <h2 class="section-title">2. Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>
  <p class="section-objective">Quantify and visualize the relationship between book price and user rating. Determine whether higher prices are associated with higher or lower ratings.</p>

  <div class="chart-block">
    <img src="{charts['scatter']}" alt="Price vs Rating Scatter Plot">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 2: Scatter plot of Price vs. User Rating for all 550 records, with linear trend line and Pearson correlation coefficient.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <div style="text-align:center; margin-bottom: 16px;">
        <span class="corr-display neg">r = {rd['pearson_r']:.4f} &nbsp;|&nbsp; p = {rd['pearson_p']:.4f}</span>
        <div style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin-top:4px;">Overall Pearson Correlation</div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The Pearson correlation coefficient between price and user rating is <strong>r = {rd['pearson_r']:.4f}</strong> (p = {rd['pearson_p']:.4f}), indicating a <strong>{correlation_label(rd['pearson_r'], rd['pearson_p'], rd['p_value_significant'])}</strong> correlation.</p>
        <p>In practical terms: <strong>higher prices do not translate to higher customer satisfaction</strong>. If anything, there is a slight tendency for more expensive books to receive marginally lower ratings. This challenges the assumption that price signals quality in the book market.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Metric</th><th class="num">Overall</th><th class="num">Fiction</th><th class="num">Non-Fiction</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Pearson r</td><td class="num">{rd['pearson_r']:.4f}</td><td class="num">{rd['fic_r']:.4f}</td><td class="num">{rd['nonfic_r']:.4f}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>p-value</td><td class="num">{rd['pearson_p']:.4f}</td><td class="num">{rd['fic_p']:.4f}</td><td class="num">{rd['nonfic_p']:.4f}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Significant (α=0.05)</td><td class="num">{'Yes' if rd['p_value_significant'] else 'No'}</td><td class="num">{'Yes' if rd['fic_p_significant'] else 'No'}</td><td class="num">{'Yes' if rd['nonfic_p_significant'] else 'No'}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Interpretation</td><td class="num">{correlation_label(rd['pearson_r'], rd['pearson_p'], rd['p_value_significant'])}</td><td class="num">{correlation_label(rd['fic_r'], rd['fic_p'], rd['fic_p_significant'])}</td><td class="num">{correlation_label(rd['nonfic_r'], rd['nonfic_p'], rd['nonfic_p_significant'])}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-block">
      <img src="{charts['rating_dist']}" alt="Rating Distribution">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 3: Distribution of user ratings. The narrow spread (σ={rd['rating_std']}) reflects the bestseller selection bias.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-block">
      <img src="{charts['price_dist']}" alt="Price Distribution">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 4: Distribution of book prices. Right-skewed with mean ${rd['avg_price']:.2f} > median ${rd['median_price']:.2f}.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout insight">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> The negative correlation, while weak (|r| < 0.15), is statistically significant for the overall dataset and for Non-Fiction. For Fiction, the correlation is not significant — suggesting that Fiction readers are less price-sensitive in their rating behavior. The data does <strong>not</strong> support a "you get what you pay for" narrative in bestselling books.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 3: GENRE COMPARISON ═══════════ -->
<section id="genre-comparison">
  <h2 class="section-title">3. Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing and Rating Strategies</h2>
  <p class="section-objective">Compare average price, user rating, and review counts between genres. Highlight key differences and identify which genre delivers better perceived value.</p>

  <div class="chart-block">
    <img src="{charts['genre_compare']}" alt="Genre Comparison Charts">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 5: Head-to-head comparison of Fiction and Non-Fiction across price, rating, and review volume.</div>
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th class="num">Fiction</th><th class="num">Non-Fiction</th><th class="num">Difference</th><th>Insight</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Records</td>
        <td class="num">{rd['fiction_count']} ({rd['fiction_pct']}%)</td>
        <td class="num">{rd['nonfiction_count']} ({rd['nonfiction_pct']}%)</td>
        <td class="num">{rd['nonfiction_count'] - rd['fiction_count']}</td>
        <td>Non-Fiction has more bestseller slots</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Avg. Price</td>
        <td class="num">{format_price(rd['fic_avg_price'])}</td>
        <td class="num">{format_price(rd['nonfic_avg_price'])}</td>
        <td class="num" style="color:var(--nonfiction)">+${rd['nonfic_avg_price'] - rd['fic_avg_price']:.2f}</td>
        <td>Non-Fiction is 37% more expensive on average</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Avg. Rating</td>
        <td class="num">{rd['fic_avg_rating']:.3f}</td>
        <td class="num">{rd['nonfic_avg_rating']:.3f}</td>
        <td class="num" style="color:var(--fiction)">+{rd['fic_avg_rating'] - rd['nonfic_avg_rating']:.3f}</td>
        <td>Fiction rated slightly higher despite lower prices</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Avg. Reviews</td>
        <td class="num">{format_num(rd['fic_avg_reviews'])}</td>
        <td class="num">{format_num(rd['nonfic_avg_reviews'])}</td>
        <td class="num" style="color:var(--fiction)">+{format_num(rd['fic_avg_reviews'] - rd['nonfic_avg_reviews'])}</td>
        <td>Fiction drives 73% more reviews per title</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Price Std. Dev.</td>
        <td class="num">${rd['fic_price_std']:.2f}</td>
        <td class="num">${rd['nonfic_price_std']:.2f}</td>
        <td class="num">—</td>
        <td>Non-Fiction has wider price dispersion</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Price–Rating r</td>
        <td class="num">{rd['fic_r']:.4f} ({'significant' if rd['fic_p_significant'] else 'not significant'})</td>
        <td class="num">{rd['nonfic_r']:.4f} ({'significant' if rd['nonfic_p_significant'] else 'not significant'})</td>
        <td class="num">—</td>
        <td>Non-Fiction shows a significant (though weak) negative price-rating relationship</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout insight">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Fiction delivers a more favorable value proposition — <strong>lower average prices (${rd['fic_avg_price']:.2f} vs. ${rd['nonfic_avg_price']:.2f})</strong> with <strong>higher average ratings ({rd['fic_avg_rating']:.3f} vs. {rd['nonfic_avg_rating']:.3f})</strong> and substantially more reader engagement (73% higher review volume). This suggests Fiction readers are more engaged and satisfied per dollar spent, while Non-Fiction commands premium pricing without a corresponding quality signal from ratings.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 4: TEMPORAL TRENDS ═══════════ -->
<section id="temporal-trends">
  <h2 class="section-title">4. Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>
  <p class="section-objective">Analyze how average book prices, user ratings, and review volumes have changed annually from 2009 to 2019. Identify inflection points and notable shifts.</p>

  <div class="chart-block">
    <img src="{charts['yearly_trends']}" alt="Year-over-Year Trends">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 6: Annual trends in average price, user rating, review count, and genre composition (2009–2019).</div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-block">
    <img src="{charts['rating_range']}" alt="Rating Range by Year">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 7: Annual rating range (min–max band) with mean trend line, showing tightening and widening of quality dispersion over time.</div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px; font-size:1.15rem;">Pricing Trends</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Average bestseller prices fluctuated between <strong>{format_price(min_price_year['avg_price'])} ({int(min_price_year['year'])})</strong> and <strong>{format_price(max_price_year['avg_price'])} ({int(max_price_year['year'])})</strong>. The most dramatic shift was the <strong>{max_price_drop['change']:.2f} USD drop from {max_price_drop['years']}</strong>, followed by a recovery with the <strong>largest single-year increase of +${max_price_rise['change']:.2f} from {max_price_rise['years']}</strong>.</p>
    <p>Fiction pricing has been notably more stable, ranging from ${fic_min_price['fic_avg_price']:.2f} ({int(fic_min_price['year'])}) to ${fic_max_price['fic_avg_price']:.2f} ({int(fic_max_price['year')}). Non-Fiction shows more volatility, with prices swinging between ${nonfic_min_price['nonfic_avg_price']:.2f} and ${nonfic_max_price['nonfic_avg_price']:.2f} — driven by premium reference works and higher-priced non-fiction categories.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px; font-size:1.15rem;">Rating Trends</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Average ratings show a <strong>gradual upward trajectory</strong> from {min_rating_year['avg_rating']:.3f} ({int(min_rating_year['year'])}) to a peak of {max_rating_year['avg_rating']:.3f} ({int(max_rating_year['year'])}). This may reflect improving book quality, more generous rating behavior over time, or algorithmic curation by Amazon surfacing higher-rated titles to bestseller status.</p>
    <p>Notably, the <strong>largest year-over-year rating improvement was +{max_rating_rise['change']:.3f} ({max_rating_rise['years']})</strong>, while the <strong>largest decline was {max_rating_drop['change']:.3f} ({max_rating_drop['years']})</strong> — changes that are meaningful in the context of the narrow 4.3–4.9 rating band.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px; font-size:1.15rem;">Genre Composition Shifts</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The Fiction/Non-Fiction balance has not been static. Non-Fiction's share of the bestseller list expanded significantly in the early-to-mid 2010s, coinciding with the adult coloring book trend and a wave of political memoirs. Fiction maintained a more consistent presence throughout the decade.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-block">
    <img src="{charts['price_segment']}" alt="Price Segment Analysis">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 8: Books per price segment and average rating within each segment. The ${price_segment_data[0]['segment']} segment has the highest average rating at {max((s['avg_rating'] for s in price_segment_data), key=lambda x: x):.3f}.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 5: TOP AUTHORS ═══════════ -->
<section id="top-authors">
  <h2 class="section-title">5. Dominant Authors on the Bestseller List</h2>
  <p class="section-objective">Identify authors who appear most frequently on the bestseller list across the decade and analyze their pricing patterns and ratings.</p>

  <div class="chart-block">
    <img src="{charts['top_authors']}" alt="Top Authors by Bestseller Appearances">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 9: Top 15 authors ranked by total bestseller appearances (2009–2019), with average price and rating annotations.</div>
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>#</th><th>Author</th><th>Genre</th><th class="num">Appearances</th><th class="num">Unique Books</th><th class="num">Avg. Price</th><th class="num">Avg. Rating</th><th class="num">Avg. Reviews</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for i, a in enumerate(author_data, 1):
    genre_label = a['genres'].replace(',', ' / ')
    html += f'''
      <tr>
        <td>{i}</td>
        <td><strong>{a['name']}</strong></td>
        <td>{genre_label}</td>
        <td class="num">{a['appearances']}</td>
        <td class="num">{a['unique_books']}</td>
        <td class="num">{format_price(a['avg_price'])}</td>
        <td class="num">{a['avg_rating']:.3f}</td>
        <td class="num">{format_num(a['avg_reviews'])}</td>
      </tr>'''

html += '''
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>Jeff Kinney</strong> (Diary of a Wimpy Kid series) leads with 12 appearances — appearing every year — maintaining a remarkable 4.80 average rating at just $9.25 average price. His formula of affordable pricing and consistent quality demonstrates that <strong>moderate pricing paired with strong reader loyalty</strong> is a reliable path to sustained bestseller status.</p>
      <p><strong>Gary Chapman</strong> (The 5 Love Languages) and <strong>Suzanne Collins</strong> (The Hunger Games) each appear 11 times, representing Non-Fiction and Fiction respectively. Chapman commands a 86% higher average price ($17.18 vs $9.25) than Kinney with slightly lower ratings.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="callout insight">
        <strong>Pattern:</strong> Authors with the highest average ratings (Eric Carle: 4.90, Dav Pilkey: 4.90, Sarah Young: 4.90) all price their books below the market average ($5.00–$8.00). No author with an average price above $20 achieved a rating above 4.70, reinforcing the finding that <strong>price and satisfaction are not positively linked</strong> in this market.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: RECOMMENDATIONS ═══════════ -->
<section id="recommendations">
  <h2 class="section-title">6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <p class="section-objective">Synthesize findings into actionable pricing and positioning recommendations for publishers and retailers targeting the Amazon bestseller list.</p>

  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 01</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Resist Premium-Price Positioning for Ratings</div>
      <div class="rec-body">The data shows a weak but statistically significant negative correlation (r = ''' + f"{rd['pearson_r']:.4f}" + ''') between price and rating. Higher-priced books do not earn higher ratings. Publishers should not assume that premium pricing will be validated by customer satisfaction — the opposite is more likely.</div>
      <div class="rec-source">← Section 2: Correlation Analysis</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 02</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Price Fiction Competitively — Optimize for Volume</div>
      <div class="rec-body">Fiction achieves 4.65 average rating at $10.85 — outperforming Non-Fiction on both satisfaction and engagement per dollar. Fiction buyers are review-active (15,684 avg reviews vs. 9,065 for Non-Fiction). Competitive pricing amplifies discovery through review velocity.</div>
      <div class="rec-source">← Section 3: Genre Comparison</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 03</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Leverage the $0–$10 Sweet Spot for Maximum Ratings</div>
      <div class="rec-body">Books priced under $10 consistently achieve the highest average ratings. The $0–$5 segment had the highest rating average. Free/promotional pricing (12 books at $0) averaged 4.77 — above the 4.62 overall mean — suggesting promotional pricing can boost satisfaction signals without harming perceived quality.</div>
      <div class="rec-source">← Section 4: Price Segment Analysis</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 04</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Justify Non-Fiction Premiums with Perceived Authority</div>
      <div class="rec-body">Non-Fiction prices average 37% higher ($14.84 vs. $10.85) but generate lower ratings (4.60 vs. 4.65). This premium can only be sustained if the content delivers clear, differentiated value. Publishers should ensure premium Non-Fiction pricing is backed by author credentials, original research, or proprietary frameworks.</div>
      <div class="rec-source">← Sections 3 & 5: Genre and Author Analysis</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 05</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Monitor the 2014–2016 Pricing Inflection Point</div>
      <div class="rec-body">The -$4.22 price drop (2014→2015) followed by +$2.76 recovery (2015→2016) represents the most volatile pricing period in the dataset. This coincides with the adult coloring book boom and may reflect market correction. Similar dislocations are forecast opportunities — be ready to adjust pricing strategy during category fads.</div>
      <div class="rec-source">← Section 4: Temporal Trends</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 06</div>
      <div class="rec-title">Build Author Franchises at Accessible Price Points</div>
      <div class="rec-body">The top 3 most-appearing authors (Jeff Kinney, Rick Riordan, Gary Chapman) average 11+ appearances each with $9–$17 price ranges and 4.73+ ratings. Consistency and series-building at moderate price points drive repeat bestseller appearances more reliably than one-off premium titles.</div>
      <div class="rec-source">← Section 5: Top Authors</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ METHODOLOGY ═══════════ -->
<section style="border-bottom: none;">
  <h2 class="section-title">Methodology & Data Notes</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Data Source:</strong> Kaggle dataset "Amazon Bestsellers: Price vs User Rating Analysis" — 550 records representing the top 50 bestselling books on Amazon for each year from 2009 to 2019.</p>
    <p><strong>Statistical Methods:</strong> Pearson correlation coefficient (r) with two-tailed p-value for price–rating relationships. Year-over-year changes computed as arithmetic differences in annual means. Price segments defined as $0–$5, $6–$10, $11–$15, $16–$20, and $21+.</p>
    <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> (1) The dataset captures only the top 50 per year — findings apply to bestsellers, not the broader book market. (2) Causality cannot be inferred from correlation; price may correlate with unobserved variables (genre conventions, publisher size, marketing spend) that influence ratings. (3) Rating distributions are compressed (4.3–4.9), limiting the magnitude of observable correlations. (4) Duplicate entries exist by design — the same book can appear in multiple years.</p>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- .container -->

<div class="footer">
  <div class="container">
    <p>Amazon Bestselling Books: Price vs. User Rating Trend Analysis &copy; 2026</p>
    <p>Data: Kaggle / obaidhere • 550 records • 2009–2019</p>
  </div>
</div>

</body>
</html>'''

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print(f"✓ Report written to /app/output/final_report.html ({len(html):,} bytes)")

#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate the final HTML report from computed data and charts."""

import json

with open('/app/report_data.json') as f:
    data = json.load(f)

rd = data['report_data']
charts = data['charts']
yearly_data = data['yearly_data']
author_data = data['author_data']
price_segment_data = data['price_segment_data']
